<TopicPage slug="subjects/agriculture/advanced/agroecology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    By this tier you have met the input-substitution logic of industrial agriculture: yield gaps get
    closed with fertiliser, pests with chemistry, weeds with herbicide. Agroecology inverts the
    framing. Instead of importing solutions to problems the cropping system itself generates, it asks
    whether the system can be designed so those problems barely arise — nutrient cycles closed on the
    farm, pests regulated by their own enemies, weeds shaded out by competitive crops. The unit of
    design stops being the field and becomes the <strong>interaction</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is not nostalgia. Every practice below is mechanistic and testable: nitrogen fixation has a
    stoichiometry, grazing has a regrowth physiology, push-pull has volatile chemistry. What makes
    agroecology a live intellectual project rather than a doctrine is that its claims are comparative
    — against the industrial baseline on yield, resilience, labour and pollution — and those
    comparisons sometimes go both ways.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="interactions, tested patterns, the honest debate, the movement" widget="equationStep" config={agroecoCfg} />

  <h2>Extensive versus intensive pasture management</h2>
  <p>
    Grasslands feed ruminants that cannot digest grain-based diets cheaply, and the management fork
    here is the syllabus's classic. <strong>Carrying capacity</strong> is the anchor concept: how many
    animal-units a hectare sustains indefinitely without degrading. The arithmetic is a forage budget.
    A temperate pasture producing 4,000 kg of usable dry matter per hectare over a growing season, and
    a 500 kg cow eating about 12.5 kg of dry matter daily (2.5% of body weight), supplies roughly
    320 cow-days per hectare — and a competent budget deducts 30–50% before that, because residual leaf
    must remain for regrowth and some forage is trampled or refused.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Extensive</strong> management runs low stocking rates over large areas — cheap per
    hectare, essential in arid rangelands where cropland is impossible, but vulnerable to the
    tragedy-of-commons dynamic where open access invites overstocking. <strong>Intensive</strong>
    management pushes stocking rates up with inputs: fertilised leys, irrigation, and above all
    <strong>rotational (management-intensive) grazing</strong>, which subdivides pasture with fencing
    and alternates grazing with rest. The mechanism is plant physiology, not ideology: a continuously
    grazed plant is kept at a leaf-area index too low to photosynthesise profitably and drains root
    carbohydrate reserves rebuilding leaves; a rested plant restores canopy and roots. Well-executed
    rotational systems typically lift forage production 20–50% on the same land. The honest limit:
    gains vanish in low-rainfall rangelands where regrowth is rainfall-limited, where the intensive
    kit (fencing, water points, labour) costs more than the extra grass returns.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "rotational grazing works because animals move" — the movement is
    incidental; the <em>rest period</em> is the active ingredient. A rotation with too-short rests
    performs worse than continuous grazing, because every paddock gets hammered young.
  </p>

  <h2>Farming the interactions: intercropping and push-pull</h2>
  <p>
    Monocultures maximise one interaction (crop × sunlight) by eliminating the rest; intercropping
    harvests several. The classical argument is <strong>land equivalent ratio (LER)</strong>: plant
    maize and beans together, and if one hectare of intercrop yields what 1.3 hectares of sole crops
    would (LER = 1.3), the mixture has beaten the monoculture on total land productivity. The
    mechanisms are complementary resource use (deep maize roots beside shallow bean roots; a C4 cereal
    beside a shade-tolerant legume), plus legume nitrogen shared via decomposing residues.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most celebrated engineered system is <strong>push-pull</strong>, developed by ICIPE for
    smallholder maize in East Africa. Stem-borer moths are repelled from the maize stand
    ("push") by volatile chemicals emitted from an intercropped forage legume, <em>Desmodium</em>, and
    attracted ("pull") to border rows of Napier grass, whose stems trap and kill borer larvae. The
    same legume exudes allelochemicals that germinate and kill <em>Striga</em>, a parasitic weed
    devastating millions of hectares. Documented result: maize yields roughly doubling on affected
    smallholder plots, with no synthetic insecticide or herbicide — a designed ecosystem doing the
    work of three input categories. A newer adaptation pushes the same volatile logic into
    climate-stressed zones with drought-adapted companion plants.
  </p>

  <h2>Closing loops: nutrients, trees, fish</h2>
  <p>
    The fertiliser lesson showed Haber-Bosch nitrogen as a triumph with a footprint (energy cost,
    runoff, N<sub>2</sub>O). Agroecology's answer is biological N fixation: legume–rhizobium
    symbioses commonly deliver 50–200 kg N per hectare per year to the following crop — enough to
    matter, though not always enough for maize at European yield levels, which is why the paradigm
    pairs fixation with reduced demand rather than promising substitution alone. <strong>Agroforestry</strong>
    adds the vertical dimension: the Sahelian <em>Faidherbia albida</em> is a phenological marvel that
    drops its leaves during the cropping season, fertilising maize beneath its canopy without shading
    it — yields under the canopy are routinely 2–3× the open field in on-farm comparisons.
    <strong>Rice-fish co-culture</strong>, documented in Chinese paddies since at least the Han
    dynasty, closes another loop: fish eat pest insects and weeds, fertilise the rice, and pay the
    farmer in protein; field trials report large reductions in pesticide use at stable rice yields.
    Crop–livestock integration is the same design at farm scale — crop residues feed animals, manure
    feeds fields, and the farm imports almost nothing it does not export.
  </p>

  <h2>Pests and weeds as ecology, not enemies</h2>
  <p>
    Conservation biological control treats the farm landscape as habitat for the pest's predators and
    parasitoids: hedgerows, flower strips and field margins provision them with nectar and overwintering
    sites. The evidence is real but context-sensitive — meta-analyses find landscape complexity
    generally suppresses pests and raises natural-enemy abundance, yet the effect size depends on which
    enemy guild and which crop, and a margin that hosts ladybirds can also host slug refugia. The
    mature position: landscape design is a genuine yield-protection technology with distributional
    caveats, not a free lunch.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "agroecology means organic means input-free." Neither holds.
    Agroecology is defined by ecological design, not certification; many certified-organic farms are
    input-intensive monocultures (permitted copper and sulphur sprays, imported manure), while many
    agroecological farms use synthetic inputs sparingly and strategically. The distinction is
    <em>which</em> inputs the design makes necessary, not a category ban.
  </p>

  <h2>The yield question: sparing versus sharing</h2>
  <p>
    The sharpest critique is quantitative: world food demand is rising, and if agroecological systems
    yield 20–40% below input-intensive ones, the shortfall is grown somewhere — and "somewhere" is
    land converted from habitat. The <strong>land-sparing</strong> argument (Borlaug's inheritance:
    intensify hard, spare the wild) versus <strong>land-sharing</strong> (farm wildlife-friendly across
    more land) is a genuine empirical fight. Phalan and colleagues' work in Ghana and India found
    forest birds and trees generally fare better under high-yield farming with land left un-farmed;
    European studies of farmland birds often find the reverse, because those species evolved in
    low-intensity farmland. Both camps agree yields matter; they disagree about ecological baselines
    and leakage (does spared land actually stay spared?). The defensible synthesis is regional:
    sparing logic in intact-habitat frontier zones, sharing logic in ancient farmed landscapes, and
    agroecological design as the intensity strategy inside both.
  </p>

  <h2>Soil as living infrastructure</h2>
  <p>
    The soil lessons covered the physical system; agroecology treats it as managed biology.
    Cover crops and rotations are the cheapest practices in agronomy precisely because they
    compound: a winter cover (rye, vetch) prevents erosion, scavenges residual nitrogen, adds
    organic carbon on decomposition, and — when the cover is a legume — fixes 50–150 kg N per
    hectare for the following crop. Reduced tillage preserves the fungal networks
    (mycorrhizae) that extend plants' effective root systems, though the yield trade-offs are
    real: no-till on heavy soils can cost yield without drainage investment, which is why
    adoption is regional, not universal. The honest quantitative summary from long-term trials
    (Rothamsted's 170-year experiments are the archetype): organic-matter gains from best
    practice are real but slow (decades), carbon saturates, and cereal yields in organic
    systems typically run 20–30% below conventional — compensated by premium prices, lower
    input costs and the stability argument. Soil health is where agroecology's claims are most
    mechanistically solid and least magical.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked intercropping calculation</h2>
  <p>
    The land-equivalent-ratio arithmetic is worth doing once. Sole maize yields 6 t/ha; sole
    beans 2 t/ha. An intercrop (two rows maize, two rows beans) yields 4.5 t/ha of maize and
    1.2 t/ha of beans. LER = (4.5/6) + (1.2/2) = 0.75 + 0.60 = 1.35 — the intercrop needs only
    about 74% of the land that sole crops would require for the same output, a 26% land-use
    efficiency gain. The mechanisms: temporal complementarity (beans maturing in the maize
    understorey), root stratification, and the legume's nitrogen. The caveats are equally
    instructive: LER measures land, not labour (intercropping is more labour-intensive per
    hectare), not mechanisability (no combine harvests a mixed stand), and not variance —
    which is why the practice thrives in labour-rich smallholder systems and struggles in
    mechanised ones. Same biology, different institutional answers.
  </p>

  <h2>Assessment beyond yield</h2>
  <p>
    Because agroecology's claims are systemic, single-metric evaluation under-measures it.
    The multi-criteria frameworks now standard in the literature score alongside yield: input
    costs (usually lower), net margin (mixed), biodiversity (consistently higher), soil carbon
    (slow gains), labour (higher) and stability (lower yield variance — the strongest repeated
    finding). The FAO's ten-element and thirteen-principle frameworks institutionalised this
    breadth, and the scientific debate has shifted to weighting: which outcomes count, for
    whom, over what horizon — a genuinely normative question agronomy alone cannot answer, and
    the reason agroecology's evaluation will always live partly in political economy.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Resilience as a measurable claim.</strong> The strongest agroecological evidence is
    perturbation response: diversified systems (polycultures, cover crops, agroforestry) show more
    stable yields across years and better drought performance — meta-analyses consistently find
    reduced yield variance, even where mean yield is lower. Stability, not peak output, is where the
    paradigm has its strongest cards, and insurance-value framing (what a farmer would pay to cut
    downside risk) is the live economic research area.
    <strong>The evaluation problem.</strong> Agroecology is also a social movement — La Via Campesina,
    food sovereignty — and that dual identity has consequences: it mobilises adoption and funding that
    pure agronomy never would, but it also makes some trial designs politically loaded and some
    failures under-reported. Disciplined readers separate the mechanism claims (testable, mostly
    holding up) from the movement claims (normative, not empirical).
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Can legume-dominated rotations be bred to fix enough N for
    cereal-class yields? Can push-pull's volatile chemistry be generalised beyond cereal systems? Do
    soil-carbon gains in regenerative systems persist, or saturate within decades? And can
    participatory plant breeding — farmers as co-researchers — scale the way its proponents claim,
    or does it remain a village-scale technology? None of these are settled; all of them are being
    measured right now.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two developments will shape the next decade of this argument. Gene editing (the previous
    lesson's toolkit) and agroecology are usually narrated as rivals, but their intersection is
    productive: durable resistance bred into seed reduces the pesticide burden agroecology also
    targets, and ecologically designed rotations slow the evolution that defeats single-gene
    resistance — the two approaches are complements in resistance management, whatever their
    advocates' rhetoric. And the resilience evidence is moving from variance statistics to
    valuation: if diversified systems cut downside yield risk measurably, that insurance value
    can be priced (what would a farmer pay for the reduction?), and priced resilience is the
    argument that can win budgets, insurance products and subsidy design — the empirical
    translation of "stability matters" that the paradigm has always needed.
  </p>

